## Subcommands
<hr class="section-rule lvl-2">

!!! terminology "What are subcommands?"
    Subcommands[^cli] are just the typical interface style you'd see with things like `git log ..` or `docker run ..`.  
    
Support for subcommand-style CLIs exists in many languages and libraries, but it's something that's normally out of reach for Makefile.  As independent goals.. `make target1 target2` are considered without any kind of layered or shared context.  Fair enough *for build automation*, but this is a significant obstacle for general scripting!

To work around exactly this problem, `compose.mk` adds suport for subcommand-style CLIs that is flexible, powerful, and requires minimal code changes.  Subcommand registration can be discovered by namespacing, or by explicit attachment with decorator-style syntax.  Usage / help details are generated automatically.


Subcommands are tested, [dog-fooded extensively]({{_}}/cmk/compiler), and fairly mature.  But..

!!! caveats "Caveats"
    Subcommand-style entrypoints do *not* require using the [cmk-language]({{_}}/cmk/compiler), and it works with vanilla Makefile.  But for reasons related to [signals & supervisors]({{_}}/signals) subcommands *do require* using the `compose.mk` interpreter.

### Interface / Contract
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Two pieces: an **entrypoint** whose recipe calls `cli.subcommands.enter`, and one **handler
target per subcommand**.

{{site.embed_mirror('demos/subcommands.mk', 'demos/cmk/subcommands.cmk', github=github, default='left')}}

{{ site.contract_card([
  ['<entrypoint>', 'Any target whose recipe calls <code>$(call cli.subcommands.enter)</code>.  Its own name is the default <b>namespace</b>, so <code>greet</code> puts handlers under <code>.greet.*</code>.'],
  ['.<namespace>.<subcmd>', 'A <b>non-parametric</b> handler &mdash; no <code>%</code> stem, so the next CLI word is <i>not</i> consumed as an argument (trailing words still arrive in <code>argv</code>).'],
  ['.<namespace>.<subcmd>/%', 'A <b>parametric</b> handler &mdash; the next CLI word becomes its <code>%</code> stem, and any further words arrive in the <code>argv</code> environment variable.'],
], subject='cli.subcommands.enter', link=_ + '/api/#clisubcommands',
   note='The subcommand list and the <b>default</b> subcommand are detected automatically by reflecting your handlers, and usage / help is generated from them.  With no subcommand (or <code>help</code> / <code>-h</code>), usage is printed.  An <b>unrecognized first word</b> routes to the default <i>only when the default is parametric</i> (the word becomes its argument; this is what makes <code>cmk &lt;file&gt;</code> shorthand work).  If the default is non-parametric, an unrecognized word is an &ldquo;unknown subcommand&rdquo; error.') }}

### Example
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

The `greet` twins above, in use:

```bash {.cli_example}
# non-parametric subcommands
$ ./demos/subcommands.mk greet world
$ ./demos/subcommands.mk greet me

# parametric subcommand -- the word after `hello` is its argument
$ ./demos/subcommands.mk greet hello bob

# bare, `help`, or `-h` prints the generated usage below
$ ./demos/subcommands.mk greet
```

Usage is **generated from the handlers**, and marks the ones that take an argument:

```bash {.cli_output}
greet // USAGE: greet <subcommand> [args..]
  ├─world
  ├─me
  ╰─hello <arg> [args..]
```

### Entrypoint Body
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

The entrypoint is usually just the lone `cli.subcommands.enter` macro call, but its
recipe body is ordinary and can do more:

* **Docstring.**  A `@#` docstring on the entrypoint target is surfaced as a description at
  the top of the generated help (try `greet help`).  Keep it short; the subcommand list
  itself is generated.
* **Preamble.**  Recipe lines *before* the `enter` call run on every invocation, before
  dispatch, which is handy for a banner or a preflight check (say, verifying a required
  tool is on the host).  Caveats: the preamble also runs for `help`/unknown invocations; environment it
  exports does **not** reach the dispatched subcommand (which runs in a separate process);
  and lines *after* `enter` never run (it transfers control to the dispatcher).

### Keyword Arguments
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

`cli.subcommands.enter` reflects everything it needs from your handlers, but each piece is
overridable with a keyword argument:

| kwarg | default | meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `namespace` | the target name | one or more (space-separated) namespaces to search |
| `subs` | reflected from the handlers | the subcommand list |
| `default` | the first subcommand | the bare-form fallback subcommand |
| `sep` | `.` | separator between namespace and subcommand in a handler name |

{% set force_default_mk %}
# e.g. force a specific default
greet:; $(call cli.subcommands.enter, default=hello)
{% endset %}
{% set force_default_cmk %}
# e.g. force a specific default
@cli.subcommands(default=hello)
greet:
{% endset %}
{{ site.inline_mirror('mirror_force_default', force_default_mk, force_default_cmk, default='left') }}

Passing kwargs to the `@cli.subcommands` decorator opts out of the tree-glyph sugar (so
handlers use the plain `.<namespace>.<subcmd>` names); a *bare* `@cli.subcommands` keeps the
`├─`/`╰─` tree form shown in the twin above.

!!! tip "Namespaces & MRO"
    Because `namespace` accepts *several* namespaces, searched in order, we land not far from
    the idea of MRO *(=method resolution order)* in object-oriented programming.  See [this
    demo]({{_}}/demos/oop/#subcommands-as-oop) for more on that aspect.

